{"name":"Oberon","hex":"#fcd389","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Oberon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A fictional character in medieval and Renaissance literature, the king of the fairies, appearing for example in William Shakespeare's play A Midsummer Night's Dream.","The outermost major moon of Uranus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Oberon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oberon","blocks":[{"text":"Oberon is a king of the fairies in medieval and Renaissance literature. He is a character in William Shakespeare's play A Midsummer Night's Dream, in which he is spouse of Titania, Queen of the Fairies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Joseph_Noel_Paton_-_The_Reconciliation_of_Titania_and_Oberon.jpg/330px-Joseph_Noel_Paton_-_The_Reconciliation_of_Titania_and_Oberon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon","wikidata":"Q1139234"},{"title":"Oberon (moon)","blocks":[{"text":"Oberon is the outermost and second-largest major moon of the planet Uranus. It is the second-most massive of the Uranian moons, and the tenth-largest moon in the Solar System. Discovered by William Herschel in 1787, Oberon is named after the mythical king of the fairies who appears as a character in Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream. Its orbit lies partially outside Uranus's magnetosphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Oberon_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg/330px-Oberon_in_true_color_by_Kevin_M._Gill.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)","wikidata":"Q3332"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/The_Song_of_Los_copy_B%2C_object_5_by_William_Blake.jpg/500px-The_Song_of_Los_copy_B%2C_object_5_by_William_Blake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_